[38;2;124;124;124mThe classic construction: arrange n² distinct numbers in n blocks of n. Within [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meach block, the numbers are decreasing. Across blocks, the numbers are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mincreasing (each block's values are all larger than the previous block's).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the sequence is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBlock 1: n, n-1, ..., 1 (decreasing)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBlock 2: 2n, 2n-1, ..., n+1 (decreasing)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m...[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBlock k: kn, kn-1, ..., (k-1)n+1 (decreasing)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m...[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBlock n: n², n²-1, ..., n²-n+1 (decreasing)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the full sequence is: n, n-1, ..., 1, 2n, 2n-1, ..., n+1, ..., n², n²-1, ...,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mn²-n+1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow I need to verify:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. No increasing subsequence of length n+1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. No decreasing subsequence of length n+1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor increasing subsequence: An increasing subsequence can take at most one [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melement from each block (since within a block, elements are decreasing). There [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare n blocks, so at most n elements. Thus no increasing subsequence of length [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn+1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor decreasing subsequence: A decreasing subsequence must have all elements from[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe same block (since elements in later blocks are all larger than elements in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mearlier blocks, so you can't pick a decreasing element from a later block after [0m[38;2;124;124;124mone from an earlier block). Wait, actually: if we pick an element from block i [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand then an element from block j > i, the element from block j is larger (since [0m[38;2;124;124;124mblock j's values are all greater than block i's values). So for a decreasing [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubsequence, once we pick from block i, we can only pick from block i or earlier[0m[38;2;124;124;124mblocks. Actually, we need decreasing, so each subsequent element must be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msmaller. Elements in later blocks are larger, so we can't go to a later block. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo all elements in a decreasing subsequence come from the same block. Each block[0m[38;2;124;124;124mhas n elements, so the longest decreasing subsequence has length n. Thus no [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdecreasing subsequence of length n+1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis proves the theorem is sharp.[0m

